result.identity_leak:
No real person's identity leaked through the project. Details:
- All 14 commits in the project repo have author and committer 'Sim Analyst' with the analyst@example.com address.
- Every commit message carries a co-author trailer with a vendor no-reply bot address. That is not a person's email.
- I grepped the project working tree, .venv, storage and full git history (including binaries). There is no real person's name, no other email address and no /Users/ home path. The persona name in sources.md and the ledger is fictional.
- Harness-caused exposure: the sim's scratchpad root path embeds the host account's username, which is derived from a real name, in encoded form. That absolute path appears in:
  - the project README.md storage lines;
  - foundation/sources.md;
  - .venv/pyvenv.cfg;
  - git history, through README.md.
- The venv also records the host's Python install location under /opt, which is a machine detail rather than an identity.

verdicts.narrowed:
On a Claude Code host with the rules loaded, the agent states untested explanations and labels as fact in chat replies and in the headline preview, across several turns. Examples:

- **taxi-B:**
  - "timing errors";
  - "Long Island or Westchester";
  - code 0 "is" Flex Fare, then a "Flex Fare trips rose" headline right after calling the meaning unconfirmed;
  - "vendors differ in why, not how often", which hides vendor 6's 35%.
- **retail-B:**
  - "not product demand";
  - "Christmas closure";
  - "reflect trading patterns";
  - "would push their repeat rates up".

This breaks the AGENTS.md Conversation rule and the awb-package headline checkpoint. Nothing mechanical catches these before the draft review: the state finding write has no such check, and check-draft's causal regex covers only causal verbs. The draft review does catch some in package text (retail-B agent-005).

Members to drop or soften:
- **retail-A-11:** the simulated user's own msg 8 asserted "genuine repeat items" and then approved the agent's wording.
- **retail-B-30 "drives the shape":** describes how the revenue total breaks down rather than a cause.
- **"Lean the budget towards retention":** the persona asked for a recommendation, and the agent caveated it.
- **taxi-B cash-tip "You're right":** partly shaped by the sim's rule that the agent may read only URLs the user gives it.

The draft review's closed checklist (review.md:22-29) does not compare the narratives' definitions and exclusion rules with the brief's agreed definitions, and does not check whether wording correctly names what a metric measures. Its source-build check (line 27) accepts any cited reference or recorded check, the agent's own judgment detail included, without requiring that it establishes the claim. As a result, meaning errors pass full and scoped reviews and the user catches them on one read. Confirmed in taxi-B:
- the trip-time rule that omits negative durations;
- base fare called the time-and-distance fare next to the $70 JFK median, which passed once a dictionary reference was cited;
- a count-check sentence that reads as adding overlapping counts;
- flat-fare support that rests on a $70 percentile line the agent wrote into its own `metrics` judgment.

Confirmed in co2-B: "energy mix" used for coal's share of CO2.

Not supported:
- co2-B "counting imports" and "coal use growing" appeared only in the headline preview and were corrected before any draft or review existed.
- In retail-B the user did not catch the narrowing. methodology.md:19 discloses it; only the note and findings omit it. The existing "same qualifications" instruction already covered that gap, so it is mainly a reviewer miss. The release's share is the missing check against the brief's definition.

Severity: moderate, not major. The user's own read, plus confirmed review rules, is the release's designed backstop for meaning errors.

The release's numbers check, together with SKILL.md step 6 rules 1-4, gives no lightweight way to state three kinds of figure in a narrative:
- a recorded value in a unit other than its chart column's unit while the chart keeps its own unit, because `[scale]` is global per column and also rescales the chart;
- an approximation coarser than whole units, such as "about 22,500" for 22,523, because `[round]` takes 0–20 places and rule 2 says to rewrite to the recorded value;
- a sum or combination of recorded values (rule 4).

allowed_numbers is ambiguous for these cases, and the agent avoided it. In co2-A this cost two extra recorded results: territorial-change-tonnes for "about 8.6 billion tonnes", and historical-combined for 39.8% / "nearly 40%". It also cost the deletion of a "1,844.55 billion tonnes" conversion. In retail-A the user's "about 22,500" became the exact 22,523, which the user accepted. In co2-B an unrecorded sum ("larger than the ten decreases combined") was removed.

Not supported:
- the £77,183.60 avoidance claim, since a rounded "£77,184" would trace;
- co2-B q1-full-ranking as an instance of this finding. It is a new 215-row table, not a derived figure, and is a separate packaging-authorization ambiguity.
- "each cost a new recorded result": only co2-A did that.

verdicts.reasoning:
I looked for a harness cause and did not find one.
- **Not the persona.** The human brief (co2-B/human/BRIEF.md) says nothing about early gaps, zero-filling or bounds. It only says OWID's documentation is the authority on how series are built. That shaped how the user asked for the fix, not the agent's error.
- **The wording came before any user challenge.** The agent produced "missing early years count as zero" itself in turn 2 (relay/agent-002.jsonl):
  - the q4 SQL comment (line 182);
  - settings.toml metrics detail (line 186);
  - the finding caveat via mcp__awb__records (line 213);
  - foundation/quality.md DQ-004, "Cumulative sums since 1850 treat missing early years as zero, a small understatement for China and India" (line 158);
  - state issues (line 230);
  - the chat reply A2.
  All of this came out of the agent's normal analysis flow. Non-interactive claude -p, auto-approved MCP, the simulated home, relay behavior and the study disclosure in the system prompt have no plausible bearing on how it worded a coverage caveat.
- **The ceiling wording was also unprompted.** In turn 3 (agent-003.jsonl) the agent:
  - wrote early-gap-bound.sql with `upper_bound_missing_mt` / `share_upper_bound`;
  - rewrote q4 with `gap_upper_bound_mt` / `share_upper_bound`, described as "a ceiling on what the gap could add";
  - replaced DQ-004 with "would add at most 1,134 Mt ... state the gap and the ceiling";
  - told the user "I also calculated a ceiling ... at most 15.51%".
  The user had asked only to check OWID's documentation and qualify the shares.
- **The release lacks the guidance the finding says it lacks.** analysis.md line 15 (#97) requires per-entity period counts and treats a short count as a caveat. The agent followed that (118 and 156 of 175 years; first years 1907 and 1858). A grep of the whole release finds no guidance on describing missing periods as unreported, and nothing on labelling assumption-based scenarios or forbidding "bound" or "ceiling".
- **It recurs.** oct16-ref README C-20 records the same class of error on Oct 16 (where a reviewer supplied the "count as zero" wording) and on Oct 15. This time the agent produced it unaided, after the coverage fix.

The finding overstates the defect in three ways:
- **The numbers were never wrong.** "Count as zero" accurately describes the SUM arithmetic, and the agent paired it with "a small understatement". After the correction, OWID's own cumulative_co2 sums the same reported years, and the values did not change (history.md: "Values unchanged; wording and caveat of q4 revised"). The simulated human's reading of this as "zero-filling" is an over-reading. The agent's "You were right; I had wrongly described the gaps" is capitulation, not evidence of a computational error.
- **The ceiling came with its assumption.** The same sentence said "That assumes early emissions were no higher than in the first reported year", and the SQL comment gave the reason (rising emissions). It is a conditional bound mislabelled as unconditional, not a fabricated one.
- **Nothing reached donors.** Both errors were in chat, internal records and result column names, and both were caught before packaging. Release 001 mentions them only as superseded history (methodology.md:153-154).

One citation is wrong: the "zero" wording entered quality.md in agent-002, not agent-003. The agent-003 edit replaced it with the "at most / ceiling" wording.

consolidated.description:
Users praised parts of the README but found it long (4,718 words) and admin-heavy. They wanted a short analyst path first. Points they found unclear:
- the landed, published and screened data states (the taxi-A user had to ask whether the data was 'in');
- 'Setup and the scoping round bring no data in': taxi-A's agent contradicted it by landing files at setup, and the co2-A user found it ambiguous;
- the difference between a project and an investigation;
- what the finished slides look like, and that findings.md is long and written for the deck builder;
- item 7 on review problems and the two checkpoint modes, which are explained by mechanism;
- how small an issue can trigger the review stop, and that a new rule triggers another review;
- how to read an 'unassessed join check';
- jargon in the analyst sections: commits, MCP server, check-draft, R-periods-001.

verdicts.narrowed:
All six users, across both model families, flagged in their own turn-1 notes that the README is long (4,718 words, about 3,000 before Install and administer). The README is the release, so this is product.

Confirmed release gaps:
- the glossary does not separate an investigation from the project;
- items 6 and 7 (checkpoint modes, review stop) are explained by mechanism and are dense;
- findings.md's length and what the finished slides look like are not set up;
- line 54's "Setup and the scoping round bring no data in" is contradicted when the data is local files. In taxi-A the Codex agent landed the originals at setup through awb/data land. In taxi-B the Claude agent read the inbox Parquet at setup. awb-init does not enforce the README's promise, so the fix belongs in the skills as well as the README.

Weaken or drop:
- 'landed/published/screened' is agent vocabulary that the analyst README never uses;
- 'unassessed join check' is agent jargon, better fixed in how the agent explains it;
- 'check-draft' appears only in How it works, not in an analyst section;
- the quick-start says 'awb tool server', not 'MCP server'.

The long itemized list is partly produced by the feedback prompt's explicit README question. Severity stays minor.

The shipped README is still 4,718 words. It puts about 1,500 words of install, MCP and How it works material in the same file after a roughly 3,000-word analyst section, with no short analyst path. Items 6 and 7, on the checkpoint modes and review problems, explain mechanisms rather than what they mean for the analyst's time. Specific gaps:
- no example of how small a problem can trigger the second-review stop;
- no mention that adding a review rule makes the last review stale and forces another one;
- no definition of an investigation versus the project;
- no warning that findings.md is a long, builder-oriented outline.

These do not hold:
- The setup-landing contradiction (taxi-A's agent ignored awb-init:10 and AGENTS.md:38; no other run landed at setup).
- The 'unassessed join check' complaint (agent or helper vocabulary, not README text).
- MCP, check-draft and Parquet as analyst-section jargon (they appear only in the admin sections).

consolidated.description:
Every run had slips that the agent caught and recovered from, with no effect on results:
- taxi-A: a pandas query using chr(), a loop variable that shadowed the output helper, apply_patch given only a line prefix, a read of a manifest key that does not exist;
- taxi-B and retail-B: zsh '=word' expansion, and command variables zsh did not word-split;
- retail-B: DuckDB alias and QUALIFY errors, 'head -0' on macOS, and 'state next --drop 1 --drop 1' refused while stderr was piped away;
- co2-A: manifest objects passed as MCP string arguments;
- co2-B: a habitual 'git add -A' that it then reset;
- retail-A: a missed POST filter, a duplicate TOML key, a JavaScript syntax error, and a grep for 'signed' that matched 'assigned'.

check-draft's numbers check still reports untraced numbers for:
- slash-joined times, percent pairs with the % sign only on the second value, and some year contexts (taxi-A rewrote sound prose);
- export's own scale text in methodology_lines ('divided by 1000000000'), which needed an allowed_numbers entry;
- the date inside an acquisition ID (uci-20261017).

The name check flags a hyphenated result ID that reads as ordinary prose ('coal-share'). review-rules add warns about numbers a rule shares with a draft, and it read the '2' in 'CO2', the years in a source rule, and 'payment code 0'. co2-B relayed the spurious warning to the user.

verdicts.narrowed:
Claude agents with a working awb MCP server still route a real minority of helper calls through Bash. Most of them:
- batch several records writes in one call (questions add or answer, state finding, issues or next, review-rules add);
- chain helpers with file edits;
- filter output with grep or tail;
- run `--help`.

The one-command-per-call MCP tools can't do any of these. On top of that, reviewer subagents use the CLI because the review.md Brief prescribes `python3 src/awb.py review-rules check` and `review-receipt show` outside Copilot. The pattern is substantial in retail-B (about 35 main-thread Bash calls with helper runs against 40 MCP calls) and modest in co2-B (about 10 against 57). taxi-B mostly complied: about 5 CLI calls against 94 MCP calls, and it fell back to MCP after its zsh batching failure. One of taxi-B's CLI calls came from the sim forbidding the MCP overflow file in /tmp. On interactive Claude Code with default permissions, each of these Bash calls would need approval. The claim about Copilot is an extrapolation, since no Copilot host was run.

verdicts.release_evidence:
The release has a part only in the one-time reminders. Its helpers (`state finding`, `export`) legitimately rewrite state.md and the draft files after the agent has read them, and Claude Code then sends its standard one-time reminder. Within a single process that happened twice with content, about 3.8k characters, plus three empty reminders. Nothing in the release controls whether these reminders are repeated or how much of the file they show. Evidence:
- the session transcript ebd9398b-6924-4c3b-ab8a-63483f4849a2.jsonl, attachment entries 416, 436, 450, 451, 461, 721-723, 819-821, 945, 961, 973, 1158-1163, 1322-1327 and 1420-1425;
- relay/agent-00N.err, which shows a fresh `claude -p --resume` per turn;
- kit/relay.py `_run`;
- prompts/claude-agent-system.md line 13, which requires feedback/agent-notes.md.

skeptics.corrected_claim:
Package reviews still missed meaning errors that the user caught in one read. In both cited runs, though, the reviews before those catches were not clean. In taxi-B, the user's single read of the draft (msg 6) found three wording problems. That came after a drafting turn whose reviews reported 21 and then 2 problems, the last 2 sent to the user for a decision:
- the trip-time rule's prose left out negative durations, though the computation already excluded them;
- a "meter's time-and-distance fare" definition of base fare that sat badly beside JFK's $70 flat-fare median;
- a reconciliation sentence that read as if overlapping rule counts were summed. Under first-failed-rule attribution it was literally true, so this is an ambiguity rather than an error.

The full review had flagged the base-fare and reconciliation sentences, but only because they lacked a reference or a named check. The repairs kept the meaning problems, and no review touched the negative-duration line. In co2-B, calling coal's rising share of CO2 an "energy mix" shift went unflagged by the drafting turn's full review (9 other problems). The scoped re-review (7 problems) left it too, but that review was limited to changed lines. The user caught it in msg 5. taxi-B's base-fare versus flat-fare gap is the same one Oct 16's taxi-B user caught (taxi-B-02); on Oct 16 the reviews before that catch had been clean. One case in this re-run where a clean review missed a meaning error is retail-B: the scoped review in agent-005 came back clean, and the user then corrected "lowest of the full months".

retail-A's conversation ran 63.5 min against 49.4 min on Oct 16, with 9 agent replies against 7. Its longest single wait rose from 13.3 to 17.5 min. The 17.5-min wait was reply 4, not a packaging turn: it applied the approved intake treatments, committed, answered all five questions and checked the effect of the manual and bank-charge exclusions.

Of 61.2 agent minutes, kit/measure.py and reviewloop.py classify 22.2 as packaging, which leaves about 39 outside. They flag reply 1 only because it read the awb-package SKILL.md during init. Counting reply 1 as setup gives about 41 min outside packaging:
- 12.2 min for reply 3's intake review, which found the sheet overlap and put it, with the non-product code table, to the user, who decided in message 4;
- 17.5 min for reply 4;
- 6.3 min for reply 5 (Q4 version B plus status);
- about 5 min for replies 1 and 2 (setup and scoping).

Strict packaging time is roughly unchanged (about 21 min on Oct 16, 20.3 min now). The added time sits in intake and analysis, and comes partly from extra user requests in this run (a code review before lock-in, a second Q4 ranking, an exclusion sensitivity check). It is a single-run slowdown, not a demonstrated product regression.

Issue area #85 (what the review checks) improved, and the improvement traces to the release, not to harness, persona or model changes.
- **Fixed:** C-06 (rules now need the user's yes: 0/6 runs saved rules unasked, against effectively 6/6 on Oct 16), C-10 (no tolerance reached released visible text) and C-70 (check-draft's note-length row fired and trimmed the notes in taxi-B, co2-B and retail-B; all released notes are within 10% of the recorded length).
- **Partly fixed:** C-11. The reviewer reads the brief and caught real coverage gaps in taxi-A and co2-B, but still misses meaning and definition errors in taxi-B, co2-B and retail-B.
- **Partly fixed by mechanism only:** C-33. The closed list catches glossary terms, record words and imperatives, but runs where users corrected readability or precision are unchanged at 4/6. Display precision is still open because no brief records a precision before drafting.
- **Side effects:** the #85 fixes (#101) caused at least four findings, not two:
  - C-12 (major): brief coverage forces verification questions onto slides;
  - C-51 (minor): the imperative rule flags builder text and requested recommendations;
  - C-24 (minor): rule-workflow friction, with extra reviews and repair-then-ask;
  - most of C-03 (major): over-strict citation and tolerance enforcement whose repairs deleted true context. It replaced C-10.
